<Chapter id="levy" label="Put the levy in the rules" />

## Put the levy in the rules

Your hands were always going to tire. So take you out of it: make the levy part of the
room's physics, not a hero's reflexes.

First, see the tool alone — freeze the trading.

<TaxOnlyDemo />

<Chapter id="tax-against-trade" label="Tax against trade" />

## Tax against trade

Now both rules run at once: every trade pushes the room toward one owner, every levy pulls
it back toward equal. Who wins depends on the two dials.

Physicists have a picture for questions like this: a <em>phase map</em>. One axis per dial;
every point on the map is a possible world; the color says how that world ends. Water has
one — pressure against temperature, ice against steam. Here is an empty one for our room:
stake against tax. You fill it in.

<!--
  WIDGET: PhaseDiagram (R7, owner review 2026-07-08: "let the user play with the two params,
  and use that to populate phase diagram… teach them what phase diagram is") — the reader
  runs live rooms (SandboxWorld, fresh dice, ~2.3 s each) with snap-to-grid stake/tax dials;
  each finished room paints its own cell with its measured tail-mean Gini. After three
  plays, "fill in the rest" completes the 10×13 grid live in-page (two fixed seeds per
  remaining cell, chunked under a frame budget; cached per session). White contour =
  MEASURED Gini 0.5. Dashed curve = FITTED τ* ≈ c·β² (scripts/phase-calibrate.ts,
  research/phase.ts; stability notes in notes/research/interventions.md). GATE discipline:
  "fitted", never "theoretical"/"law".
-->
<PhaseDiagram />

One map, two regimes. Below the frontier, the stake outruns the levy and the room slides
toward its single owner — slower, but surely. Above it, the levy holds. And the frontier
itself is not a straight trade-off: double the stake and you need roughly <em>four times</em>
the tax to stand still.

## The sandbox

Everything you have seen was this one machine wearing different costumes. Here it is with
every dial exposed: people, stake, a levy — and the room itself answers to your finger:
tap anyone to tax them on the spot. Break it. Save it. See what it takes.

